#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""memo-runner-dispatch: the only component that starts or stops memo-job@<id>.service.

Phase 4 (VPS runner), design decision D3 of .agent/project/phase-4/spec.md. The runtime runs as
the unprivileged user memo-runner and cannot start units itself (no polkit, NoNewPrivileges
blocks sudo). It hands requests over through a spool (contract shared with
server/runners/executor-systemd.mjs):

    /var/lib/memo-runner/spool/queue/<id>.json   start request (strict schema, see below)
    /var/lib/memo-runner/spool/cancel/<id>       stop request (empty, or a small JSON body)

<id> is the dispatch id, `^[a-z0-9-]{8,64}$` (REQ-P4-11; the executor derives it from the job
id), and the unit is memo-job@<id>.service. The request carries no argv: the recipe name selects
the allowlisted argv shapes (built in, narrowed by runner.json), which the dispatcher pins into
the unit's environment; the job-runner inside the unit must run one of them or nothing.

memo-runner-dispatch.path watches both folders and starts memo-runner-dispatch.service, a root
oneshot that runs this script. memo-runner is treated as untrusted (risk R-P4-08): every entry
is re-validated here, the unit properties come from root-owned files only, and anything that
does not match exactly is refused with `dispatch_refused`, journaled, and removed from the
spool. Nothing in a spool entry is ever passed to a shell, and no command is built from it:
the only processes started are `systemctl` with fixed verbs and a unit name derived from a
validated dispatch id.

What a valid start request does, in order:
  1. checks that no memo-job@*.service is active, activating, deactivating or has a pending
     job (concurrency 1, D4; the runtime store owns the queue, this is the second check);
  2. creates /var/lib/memo-runner/jobs/<id>/ (root:memo-runner 0751), its out/ folder
     (memo-job:memo-runner 0750) and its cache/ folder (memo-job:memo-job 0750, named by the
     install unit's ReadWritePaths=, so it must exist before that unit starts),
     and writes jobs/<id>/dispatch.json (root, 0640);
     a refusal for a well-formed id writes jobs/<id>/status.json with
     service_result "dispatch_refused" instead, which the executor reads as the job's end;
  3. writes two runtime drop-ins, built only from validated values:
     /run/systemd/system/memo-job-install@<id>.service.d/50-dispatch.conf (phase 1, dependency
     install: WorkingDirectory=, ReadWritePaths=, TimeoutStartSec=, Environment= with the fixed
     install argv shapes) and /run/systemd/system/memo-job@<id>.service.d/50-dispatch.conf
     (phase 2, the recipe: the same plus RuntimeMaxSec=, PrivateNetwork=yes and
     MEMO_JOB_NETWORK=private), and checks with `systemctl show -p DropInPaths` that systemd
     sees both (a `daemon-reload` is issued once if it does not);
  4. runs `systemctl start --no-block memo-job@<id>.service`; the template's Requires= and
     After= on memo-job-install@<id>.service run the install first, and the recipe only after
     a successful install.

A cancel writes the root-owned marker jobs/<id>/cancel.json (read by record-result, so a
stopped job ends `cancelled` or `budget_exceeded` instead of looking like a clean exit) and
runs `systemctl stop --no-block memo-job@<id>.service memo-job-install@<id>.service`. A cancel for a request still in the
queue removes it, and the job ends `cancelled_before_start` without any unit.

`--status` prints a JSON summary (queue depth, active job, free disk, last result) and
changes nothing.

systemd references (systemd 257, Debian 13; man pages read on the VPS on 2026-10-14):
  - systemd.unit(5), "drop-in" directories: for an instance foo@bar.service, systemd reads
    foo@bar.service.d/ as well as foo@.service.d/, from /run/systemd/system among others.
    Whether a never-loaded instance picks up a new drop-in WITHOUT daemon-reload is inferred,
    not documented: hence the DropInPaths check and the single daemon-reload fallback
    (to verify in the TODO-P4-01 spike).
  - systemd.service(5), RuntimeMaxSec=: "If this is used and the service has been active for
    longer than the specified time it is terminated and put into a failure state". A bare
    number is seconds (systemd.time(7)).
  - systemd.exec(5), ReadWritePaths=: "if paths contain symlinks, they are resolved". The
    worktree is checked to contain no symlink, but memo-runner owns it and could swap it
    after the check; DAC still bounds what memo-job can write (to verify in TODO-P4-01).
  - systemctl(1), list-jobs [PATTERN...], set-property, daemon-reload.

Exit status: 0 when the spool was processed (refusals included) or another dispatch held the
lock; 2 when the configuration or the spool layout is not trusted (nothing is touched); 1 on
an unexpected error.
"""

